@node D-Bus Architecture
@section Architecture

How the cmacs D-Bus subsystem is wired internally.  Useful when
hacking on it.

@subsection File layout

@example
cmacs/dbus/
+-- cmacs-dbus.c                 top-level lifecycle, dual bus name
+-- cmacs-dbus.h                 public API for in-tree consumers
+-- cmacs-dbus-internal.h        cross-module declarations
+-- cmacs-dbus-helpers.c         lisp-escape, lisp-quote, error helpers
+-- cmacs-dbus-emit.c            signal-emit C helpers + DEFUNs
+-- cmacs-dbus-properties.c      Properties iface + lazy property registry
+-- cmacs-dbus-object-manager.c  ObjectManager iface + child registry
+-- cmacs-dbus-iface-eval.c      back-compat org.cmacs.Editor1
@end example

Phases 2--5 add more @file{cmacs-dbus-iface-*.c} modules, plus
per-resource object files (@file{cmacs-dbus-buffer-obj.c}, etc.) and
integration files (@file{cmacs-dbus-search-provider.c},
@file{cmacs-dbus-mpris.c}, ...).  The pattern is: one file per
interface or per integration target.

@subsection Module registration model

Each module exposes a register/unregister pair declared in
@file{cmacs-dbus-internal.h}.  The top-level
@code{cmacs-dbus.c::register_modules} calls each enabled module's
@code{register} function in sequence after the connection is up.
Failures abort the whole start.  @code{unregister_modules} tears
down in reverse order on stop.

Each module owns its own introspection XML (no monolithic blob) and
its own @code{GDBusInterfaceVTable}.  They register on the same
path (@code{/org/cmacs/Editor}) but with distinct iface names,
which GDBus merges automatically for @code{Introspectable}.

@subsection Dispatch model

@subsubheading Method handlers

Every method body looks roughly like:

@enumerate
@item
@code{g_variant_get} the input args from @code{parameters}
@item
Build an elisp expression as a @code{gchar *}
@item
Call @code{cmacs_dispatch_eval (expr, &err)} (or one of its
specialised wrappers in @file{cmacs/glib/cmacs-eval-dispatch.c})
@item
Either @code{g_dbus_method_invocation_return_value} with the result
or @code{cmacs_dbus_return_gerror} on error
@end enumerate

The elisp emission pattern is identical to the cmacsgi handlers in
@file{cmacs/api/cmacs-api-cmd-*.c} --- same
@code{cmacs_dbus_lisp_escape} / @code{cmacs_dbus_lisp_quote} helpers,
same eval routing.  Two transports, one source of truth.

@subsubheading Why eval-routing instead of direct DEFUN calls

Three reasons:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Single source of truth.} Every cmacsgi command has an elisp
emitter; D-Bus reuses it verbatim.
@item
@strong{Long-tail coverage.} The @code{Eval} method already handles
every possible cmacs operation.  Typed methods are an ergonomic
layer on top, not a replacement.
@item
@strong{No duplicate type marshalling.} Building elisp strings is
simpler than emitting Lisp_Object directly with proper GC
protection.
@end enumerate

@subsubheading GMainContext integration

D-Bus uses GLib's main loop.  cmacs has its own GMainContext
(@code{cmacs_glib_get_context}) that's attached to the Emacs
@code{pselect()} loop via @file{cmacs-glib-loop.c}.  We push that
context as thread-default before creating the GDBus connection so
all dispatch runs there.

This means D-Bus method handlers fire from the same place as GLib
timeouts and idle callbacks: on the Emacs main thread, between
input cycles, with the @code{waiting_for_input} guard from
@code{cmacs_glib_dispatch} already in place.

@subsection Bus name acquisition

@code{cmacs-dbus-start} claims two names in this order:

@enumerate
@item
Connect to the session bus (synchronous ---
@code{g_dbus_connection_new_for_address_sync}).
@item
Register every enabled module's interface vtable.
@item
Claim per-PID name with @code{REPLACE | DO_NOT_QUEUE} (always
succeeds since per-PID is unique).
@item
Optimistically claim the well-known name with @code{DO_NOT_QUEUE}.
The @code{on_well_known_lost} callback fires immediately if another
cmacs already owns it; we clear @code{bus_name_well} in that case
so dominant-name queries fall back.
@end enumerate

The well-known acquisition is intentionally lossy: another cmacs
holding the name doesn't block startup, just turns off "primary
daemon" mode for this instance.

@subsection Properties helper internals

@file{cmacs-dbus-properties.c} maintains a three-level registry:

@example
prop_registry: GHashTable
    "/org/cmacs/Editor/Buffer/12" -> GHashTable
        "org.cmacs.Editor1.Buffer.Resource" -> GHashTable
            "Modified" -> PropEntry @{ signature, getter, setter @}
            "Filename" -> PropEntry @{ ... @}
@end example

The Properties iface vtable attaches to a path the first time
@code{cmacs_dbus_register_property} is called for that path.
@code{Get} / @code{GetAll} / @code{Set} dispatch through the
registry, evaluating the stored elisp expressions through
@code{cmacs_dispatch_eval} and type-marshalling the result via the
small parser in @code{parse_to_variant}.

Phase 1 declares no properties, so the registry is empty and the
Properties iface never attaches to any path.

@subsection ObjectManager internals

@file{cmacs-dbus-object-manager.c} maintains a two-level registry
of (path, iface) → properties.  When
@code{cmacs_dbus_object_manager_add_iface} is called, the entry is
recorded and an @code{InterfacesAdded} signal is emitted.
@code{remove_iface} deletes and emits @code{InterfacesRemoved}.
@code{GetManagedObjects} flattens the whole map into the standard
@code{a@{oa@{sa@{sv@}@}@}} reply.

Phase 1 doesn't add anything to this registry.

@subsection Build and link

@file{cmacs/dbus/} sources are added to @code{CMACS_GLIB_OBJ} in
@file{configure.ac}:

@example
CMACS_GLIB_OBJ="cmacs-glib-loop.o cmacs-gobject.o cmacs-gclosure.o \
                cmacs-eval-dispatch.o cmacs-glib-screenshot.o \
                cmacs-ink-overlay.o cmacs-dbus.o cmacs-dbus-helpers.o \
                cmacs-dbus-emit.o cmacs-dbus-properties.o \
                cmacs-dbus-object-manager.o cmacs-dbus-iface-eval.o"
@end example

The @code{src/Makefile.in::cmacs-symlinks} rule symlinks each .c
file from @file{cmacs/dbus/} into @file{src/} so the standard
@code{$(libsrc)/make-docfile} globals scanner finds them.  This is
the same pattern every other @file{cmacs/}-subdirectory uses.
The @code{CMACS_DIRS} variable in @file{src/Makefile.in} drives the
symlink search; @code{dbus} is in that list.

@subsection Adding a new interface (Phase 2+ pattern)

@enumerate
@item
Write @file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-foo.c} with: static
introspection XML for one @code{<interface>} block, a method-call
vtable function dispatching by @code{method_name}, and
@code{cmacs_dbus_iface_foo_register} / @code{unregister}.
@item
Add the prototype to @file{cmacs-dbus-internal.h}.
@item
Wire the register / unregister calls into
@code{cmacs-dbus.c::register_modules} and
@code{unregister_modules}.
@item
Append @code{cmacs-dbus-iface-foo.o} to @code{CMACS_GLIB_OBJ} in
@file{configure.ac}, run @command{autoconf} + @command{./configure}.
@item
Write tests in @file{test/cmacs/cmacs-dbus-iface-foo-tests.el}.
@end enumerate

The cmacsgi parity in Phase 3 follows this template 17 times --- once
per interface.
